66
00:03:24.872 --> 00:03:25.873
- Hey. any luck
IDing the victim?

67
00:03:26.473 --> 00:03:29.077
- Yeah. Ian Larkin. age 37.

68
00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.045
His fingerprints lit up SAFIS.

69
00:03:31.112 --> 00:03:32.714
He's a repeat customer.

70
00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.550
He's got multiple priors for
prostitution. drug possession.

71
00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.585
He's been in and out
of the system since he was 18.

72
00:03:37.685 --> 00:03:39.386
- Including a 90-day
stint last year.

73
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.089
- Any idea where he was living?

74
00:03:41.155 --> 00:03:42.657
- Last known address
in the Lower East Side.

75
00:03:42.724 --> 00:03:44.726
but the building came
down in September.

76
00:03:44.792 --> 00:03:47.260
- We did find a family address
out in South Orange. though.

77
00:03:47.294 --> 00:03:48.796
- Okay.
So Rollins and I could

78
00:03:48.863 --> 00:03:50.732
head there in the morning.
talk to the family.

79
00:03:50.765 --> 00:03:52.232
- Want me to swing by
Mount Sinai on my way home?

80
00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:53.968
- You know what?
Sorry. One sec.

81
00:03:54.035 --> 00:03:55.435
Benson.

82
00:03:55.503 --> 00:03:57.972
Mm-hmm.

83
00:03:58.039 --> 00:04:00.641
Oh. Okay.

84
00:04:00.708 --> 00:04:03.477
Yeah. thanks.

85
00:04:03.511 --> 00:04:05.947
Well. there's no need for
you to stop by the hospital.

86
00:04:06.013 --> 00:04:07.115
- Ian?

87
00:04:07.181 --> 00:04:08.516
- Dead.

88
00:04:08.583 --> 00:04:10.450
He never regained
consciousness.

89
00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:15.857
So I want to hang
onto this case.

90
00:04:15.957 --> 00:04:17.925
I'm going to call
Homicide myself.

91
00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:20.360
They're short staffed. so I'm
sure they won't mind the help.

92
00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.930
Why don't you head to
the ME's office in the morning

93
00:04:23.064 --> 00:04:26.534
and see if you can find out
what really happened to Ian.

94
00:04:29.804 --> 00:04:31.371
- Should we start with
the drugs found in the room?

95
00:04:31.438 --> 00:04:32.907
- It's your show. Doc.

96
00:04:32.974 --> 00:04:34.742
- Well. it wasn't cocaine.

97
00:04:34.809 --> 00:04:36.409
- Really? Meth?

98
00:04:36.544 --> 00:04:37.745
- Benzos.

99
00:04:37.779 --> 00:04:40.380
- So the perp drugged Ian.
knocked him out.

100
00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:41.749
- Wrong again.

101
00:04:41.783 --> 00:04:42.984
Tox screen was negative.

102
00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.252
Mr. Larkin was clean.

103
00:04:44.351 --> 00:04:45.887
Turns out he was
rendered unconscious

104
00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.287
the old-fashioned way.

105
00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.556
- Cracked on the head.

106
00:04:48.656 --> 00:04:50.224
- Hotel lamp.

107
00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:51.926
After that.
the victim was restrained

108
00:04:52.059 --> 00:04:54.294
with the zip ties. deep cuts.

109
00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:55.830
- Meaning Ian struggled.

110
00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:58.699
- And then the killer strangled
him underwater in the bathtub.

111
00:04:58.766 --> 00:05:01.569
Note the bruising
around the neck.

112
00:05:01.602 --> 00:05:05.273
- I thought Ian was alive when
the cleaning staff found him.

113
00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.975
- Best guess. he suffered
a hypoxic blackout

114
00:05:08.042 --> 00:05:10.945
while being strangled.
stopped struggling. and sank.

115
00:05:11.045 --> 00:05:12.747
- Killer assumed he was dead.

116
00:05:12.814 --> 00:05:14.182
- And after they left.

117
00:05:14.248 --> 00:05:16.617
Ian regained consciousness.
poor guy.

118
00:05:16.651 --> 00:05:19.720
- So he was just stuck
in that bathtub?

119
00:05:19.821 --> 00:05:21.255
For how long?

120
00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:23.991
- Judging by his skin
maceration. hours.

121
00:05:25.927 --> 00:05:27.394
- How are we with DNA?

122
00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.530
- Lousy. literally.

123
00:05:29.597 --> 00:05:31.599
Bargain-bin hotel
means we've got

124
00:05:31.632 --> 00:05:35.435
samples coming out our ears.
loads of cross-contamination.

125
00:05:35.536 --> 00:05:37.104
We'll run it all. but--

126
00:05:37.171 --> 00:05:39.740
- Needle in a haystack.
- Just about.

127
00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:45.378
- Ian was a good kid. kind.

128
00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:48.415
had his mother's smarts.
thank God.

129
00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:50.685
He could have done anything
he set his mind to.

130
00:05:50.785 --> 00:05:53.321
- Were the two of you close.
Louis?

131
00:05:53.453 --> 00:05:55.022
- I suppose.

132
00:05:55.156 --> 00:05:57.225
Looking back.
maybe there are things

133
00:05:57.291 --> 00:05:58.960
I should have done better with.

134
00:05:59.026 --> 00:06:04.198
- I think it always is
easier in hindsight. right?

135
00:06:04.265 --> 00:06:08.302
When was the last time
you saw Ian?

136
00:06:08.336 --> 00:06:10.137
- He was--

137
00:06:10.171 --> 00:06:14.208
I think he was
here this morning.

138
00:06:14.275 --> 00:06:17.278
Did you get to see him?

139
00:06:17.311 --> 00:06:18.913
- How long has he
been like this?

140
00:06:19.046 --> 00:06:22.683
- He's going on two years now.
went downhill after Ma died.

141
00:06:22.717 --> 00:06:24.919
another gift from Ian.
- Yeah.

142
00:06:25.052 --> 00:06:28.055
Families tend to bear
the brunt of addiction.

143
00:06:28.189 --> 00:06:29.624
- Yeah. well. my brother
was definitely

144
00:06:29.724 --> 00:06:31.959
the kind of junkie who used
and manipulated people.

145
00:06:32.059 --> 00:06:33.995
our folks. most of all.

146
00:06:34.028 --> 00:06:35.963
They lost their savings
because of him.

147
00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.164
- That's terrible.
- Yeah.

148
00:06:37.265 --> 00:06:40.001
Ma had to go back to work.
67 years old.

149
00:06:40.034 --> 00:06:41.602
put her in an early grave.

150
00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:43.771
- When did Ian start using?

151
00:06:43.838 --> 00:06:45.907
As long as I can remember.

152
00:06:46.007 --> 00:06:48.509
He started in high school.
never looked back.

153
00:06:48.609 --> 00:06:52.346
- Louis. did Ian ever
talk about his friends

154
00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:55.149
or other people in his life?

155
00:06:55.182 --> 00:06:57.818
- I think he should
be home soon.

156
00:06:57.885 --> 00:06:59.887
School will be letting out.
- Okay.

157
00:06:59.987 --> 00:07:02.056
- It's almost a relief
that Ian's dead.

158
00:07:02.156 --> 00:07:04.659
to know we don't have to pay
for his mistakes anymore.

159
00:07:04.759 --> 00:07:07.028
I guess that
makes me a bad person.

160
00:07:07.061 --> 00:07:10.665
- No. it makes you human.

161
00:07:10.765 --> 00:07:13.634
Listen. do you happen to know
where Ian was living?

162
00:07:13.768 --> 00:07:16.270
- Yeah. unfortunately.
he conned a check

163
00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:17.638
out of my dad last month.

164
00:07:17.705 --> 00:07:20.107
Let me find you the address.

165
00:07:25.513 --> 00:07:26.914
- It's a pretty
bleak existence.

166
00:07:26.948 --> 00:07:28.481
- Yeah. Welcome to the life
of an addict.

167
00:07:28.549 --> 00:07:34.188
We got supplements.
steroids. syringes.

168
00:07:34.322 --> 00:07:35.455
Got some crystal here.

169
00:07:35.589 --> 00:07:37.158
- Hey. I found a tablet.

170
00:07:37.258 --> 00:07:38.526
Battery's dead.

171
00:07:38.659 --> 00:07:41.195
Maybe we can pull
some emails or texts.

172
00:07:44.832 --> 00:07:47.134
- It looks like Ian went
shopping.

173
00:07:47.201 --> 00:07:49.870
bundle of zip ties.

174
00:07:49.937 --> 00:07:52.373
empty packaging
for a box knife.

175
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.841
- Oh. hey.

176
00:07:53.908 --> 00:07:59.113
Hey. look at this. some
bowls of crushed-up pills.

177
00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.182
Put money on
them being benzos.

178
00:08:01.248 --> 00:08:05.453
- If Ian is the one who
brought that bag to the hotel.

179
00:08:05.586 --> 00:08:07.521
what was the plan?

180
00:08:07.588 --> 00:08:09.423
- Kink gone wrong?

181
00:08:09.523 --> 00:08:10.992
Rob the John?

182
00:08:11.058 --> 00:08:12.827
- Something went sideways.

183
00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.563
- We are left with
a boatload of questions.

184
00:08:15.596 --> 00:08:17.698
- We might have
an answer to one of them.

185
00:08:17.798 --> 00:08:20.001
This is where Ian was
the day he died.

186
00:08:23.137 --> 00:08:26.707
Pro fighter expo--I gotta say.
this is a new one for me.

187
00:08:26.774 --> 00:08:28.309
- Ian was a man of culture.

188
00:08:28.376 --> 00:08:29.877
- All right. let's split up

189
00:08:29.944 --> 00:08:32.747
and see if we can find
somebody who recognizes Ian.

190
00:08:36.183 --> 00:08:37.518
What's that face?

191
00:08:37.551 --> 00:08:39.954
- That's Buffalo Joe.

192
00:08:39.987 --> 00:08:41.789
That's Hawk Hawkins
right there.

193
00:08:41.856 --> 00:08:44.091
That's Chris "The Scary"
Finnerty.

194
00:08:44.158 --> 00:08:45.359
- What's going on here?

195
00:08:45.426 --> 00:08:46.594
- I'm a fan. all right?

196
00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:48.662
When I was a kid.
I used to watch

197
00:08:48.796 --> 00:08:50.131
these fights with my uncle.

198
00:08:50.197 --> 00:08:51.332
He'd splurge on a pay-per-view.

199
00:08:51.432 --> 00:08:52.900
We'd stay up late
on a Saturday night.

200
00:08:52.933 --> 00:08:54.668
And we'd eat a bunch
of junk food. we'd watch it.

201
00:08:54.735 --> 00:08:55.936
Beat the snot out
of each other.

202
00:08:56.037 --> 00:08:56.971
It was great.

203
00:08:57.004 --> 00:08:58.339
- All right.
all right. fan boy.

204
00:08:58.439 --> 00:08:59.640
Let's keep the questions
to the case.

205
00:08:59.673 --> 00:09:01.008
- Okay. no promises.

206
00:09:01.108 --> 00:09:04.812
- Oh. and don't ask
for too many autographs.

207
00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:08.549
- Excuse me.
Hi.

208
00:09:08.616 --> 00:09:10.418
Captain Olivia Benson
with the NYPD.

209
00:09:10.484 --> 00:09:11.886
- I didn't do it.

210
00:09:11.919 --> 00:09:13.754
- I just need a moment
of your time.

211
00:09:13.821 --> 00:09:19.193
- Okay. hon. you are barking
up the absolute right tree.

212
00:09:19.293 --> 00:09:22.830
Yeah. I'm always happy
to support the babes in blue.

213
00:09:22.897 --> 00:09:24.765
- Well. thank you.
I appreciate that.

214
00:09:24.899 --> 00:09:27.334
I'm wondering if
you've seen this man.

215
00:09:27.368 --> 00:09:28.702
He might have been
in two days ago.

216
00:09:28.769 --> 00:09:30.671
- You know what I have seen?
- What?

217
00:09:30.738 --> 00:09:34.008
- I've seen that you got a
spectacular set of baby browns.

218
00:09:34.141 --> 00:09:35.309
- Well. thank you.

219
00:09:35.342 --> 00:09:36.644
That's very kind of you.

220
00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:38.245
- And those yams
aren't bad either.

221
00:09:38.379 --> 00:09:39.847
- Okay.

222
00:09:39.914 --> 00:09:41.982
- Big. big money with sticks
like that in this line of work.

223
00:09:42.083 --> 00:09:43.684
- Noted.

224
00:09:43.751 --> 00:09:46.487
- You know. I think crowds
really go for Amazonian broads.

225
00:09:46.521 --> 00:09:48.089
I think it's a dominance thing.

226
00:09:48.155 --> 00:09:49.723
But how's your right hook?

227
00:09:49.757 --> 00:09:51.492
- It's actually not bad.

228
00:09:51.526 --> 00:09:52.893
- I bet it's pretty good.
- Yeah.

229
00:09:52.927 --> 00:09:54.595
Can I--
have you seen this man?

230
00:09:54.662 --> 00:09:56.297
- Huh.
I don't know.

231
00:09:56.397 --> 00:09:57.865
Maybe. maybe not.

232
00:09:57.898 --> 00:10:01.335
I do know that
my time is valuable.

233
00:10:01.402 --> 00:10:03.003
- Really?

234
00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:05.507
All right. well.
could have fooled me.

235
00:10:05.574 --> 00:10:07.241
- Okay. all right.

236
00:10:07.308 --> 00:10:08.676
I like them spicy.

237
00:10:08.776 --> 00:10:10.444
If you're a little
low on funding.

238
00:10:10.512 --> 00:10:12.346
we can make some
kind of arrangement.

239
00:10:12.413 --> 00:10:14.748
You know. I know my way around
a pair of handcuffs.

240
00:10:14.782 --> 00:10:16.050
if that floats your boat.

241
00:10:16.117 --> 00:10:17.251
- Well. that's tempting.

242
00:10:17.351 --> 00:10:18.652
But I think today.
I'm going to--

243
00:10:18.686 --> 00:10:21.355
I'm going to stick with cash.

244
00:10:21.388 --> 00:10:23.190
Will that do it for you?

245
00:10:23.257 --> 00:10:25.826
- It'll do something.

246
00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.962
- Have you--
- Yes. I've seen your fella.

247
00:10:28.095 --> 00:10:30.664
He was here on the day
the expo opened.

248
00:10:30.698 --> 00:10:32.534
- Okay.
Did you speak with him?

249
00:10:32.601 --> 00:10:34.335
- Tried to sell him
a photo. half price.

250
00:10:34.401 --> 00:10:35.703
and the prick turned me down.

251
00:10:35.736 --> 00:10:37.972
I mean. who--in what world?
-All right.

252
00:10:38.005 --> 00:10:41.742
Did you--did you happen to
notice. was he with anyone?

253
00:10:41.809 --> 00:10:43.043
- I didn't notice. no.

254
00:10:43.077 --> 00:10:44.945
- Did you notice
where he went after?

255
00:10:45.012 --> 00:10:48.382
- Same place as everybody else.
to see the headliner.

256
00:10:48.517 --> 00:10:50.451
- Thank you very much.

257
00:10:50.519 --> 00:10:53.053
- Thank you very. very much.

258
00:10:57.024 --> 00:11:00.394
- Deuce. keep these folks
in one line. will you?

259
00:11:00.427 --> 00:11:02.329
And make sure they have cash.

260
00:11:04.498 --> 00:11:05.733
- Excuse me.

261
00:11:05.766 --> 00:11:07.201
Do you mind if I take
a peek at that?

262
00:11:07.268 --> 00:11:09.236
- Oh. yeah. sure.
- Thanks.

263
00:11:12.406 --> 00:11:13.807
- Thank you.

264
00:11:29.456 --> 00:11:31.225
- Oh.

265
00:11:35.996 --> 00:11:38.633
I can't say the kid
looks familiar.

266
00:11:38.732 --> 00:11:40.535
- He would have
been here on Friday.

267
00:11:40.569 --> 00:11:41.902
- I mean. it's possible.

268
00:11:42.002 --> 00:11:44.539
but I've seen a lot
of folks this weekend.

269
00:11:44.573 --> 00:11:45.873
And unfortunately.
the old memory

270
00:11:45.906 --> 00:11:47.542
ain't what it used to be.

271
00:11:47.609 --> 00:11:48.976
- Occupational hazard?

272
00:11:49.043 --> 00:11:51.613
- 40 years of taking
shots to the dome.

273
00:11:51.646 --> 00:11:54.583
- Yeah. I got to ask.
what's with the mask?

274
00:11:54.683 --> 00:11:56.917
- Oh. intimidation.

275
00:11:56.984 --> 00:11:59.286
Walk out to the ring
wearing something like that.

276
00:11:59.386 --> 00:12:02.557
you get in the other guy's head
before you even thrown a punch.

277
00:12:02.624 --> 00:12:04.892
Plus. the fans go nuts.

278
00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:06.561
- Mm.

279
00:12:06.628 --> 00:12:09.564
- Everybody loves a character.

280
00:12:09.631 --> 00:12:12.132
80 bucks. made in the USA.

281
00:12:12.166 --> 00:12:14.001
- You keep
the credit card receipts?

282
00:12:14.068 --> 00:12:15.637
- Cash only.

283
00:12:15.736 --> 00:12:17.672
Uncle Sam gets sticky
fingers otherwise.

284
00:12:17.738 --> 00:12:19.708
- Yeah. well. what about--
what about the photos

285
00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:20.941
that you take with fans?

286
00:12:21.008 --> 00:12:22.544
You have any digital copies?

287
00:12:22.611 --> 00:12:23.844
- Polaroids.

288
00:12:23.911 --> 00:12:26.146
I'm old-school.

289
00:12:27.448 --> 00:12:30.184
I feel like I'm kind
of striking out for you guys.

290
00:12:30.251 --> 00:12:31.686
Wish I were more help.

291
00:12:31.785 --> 00:12:33.954
- We're used to it--
occupational hazard.

292
00:12:34.021 --> 00:12:37.057
- I have to ask you where
you were on Friday night.

293
00:12:37.157 --> 00:12:38.792
- Sure.

294
00:12:38.826 --> 00:12:40.928
We wrapped up here and then--
that's right.

295
00:12:40.995 --> 00:12:42.963
And then Sharon and I.
we spent the night

296
00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:45.165
with a couple
of the bruiser fight guys.

297
00:12:45.232 --> 00:12:46.934
Oh. speaking of--

298
00:12:47.001 --> 00:12:50.037
- Sorry. I'm Sharon Huntsinger.
Clint's agent.

299
00:12:50.104 --> 00:12:52.674
The crowd's getting restless.
I really need him back.

300
00:12:52.741 --> 00:12:55.677
- Okay. well. thank you so much
for your time. Mr. O'Neill.

301
00:12:55.744 --> 00:12:57.545
And if there's anything
that you do think of.

302
00:12:57.646 --> 00:12:59.380
or anything comes back--
- Oh. yeah.

303
00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:01.282
Clint. And I will.

304
00:13:01.348 --> 00:13:02.717
Who knows?

305
00:13:02.783 --> 00:13:04.318
Maybe something
will shake loose.

306
00:13:04.351 --> 00:13:06.621
- Great.
Thank you.

307
00:13:06.688 --> 00:13:08.690
- Hey. Cap.
- Mm.

308
00:13:08.757 --> 00:13:11.091
- So off this new lens
of professional fighting.

309
00:13:11.125 --> 00:13:13.427
we pulled the paper archives.
found a mention of Ian

310
00:13:13.528 --> 00:13:14.995
in "The Post" back in 2002.

311
00:13:15.095 --> 00:13:16.830
- "Local boys looking
for a fight."

312
00:13:16.897 --> 00:13:18.533
- Yeah. so the article
talks about Ian

313
00:13:18.566 --> 00:13:19.967
and these other teen superfans.

314
00:13:20.067 --> 00:13:21.569
the Bruiser Fighting
Championship.

315
00:13:21.636 --> 00:13:25.105
It's kind of like a local MMA
precursor. got real popular.

316
00:13:25.139 --> 00:13:27.975
They would hold these
Tough Dude Tournaments. right.

317
00:13:28.042 --> 00:13:29.843
basically. like
legalized street fights.

318
00:13:29.943 --> 00:13:32.747
real big personalities.
very few rules.

319
00:13:32.781 --> 00:13:35.115
boxers like O'Neill.
judo guys. grapplers.

320
00:13:35.182 --> 00:13:37.484
And they'd all try
to kill each other.

321
00:13:37.585 --> 00:13:40.421
It got real brutal. real brutal
knockouts. lots of blood.

322
00:13:40.522 --> 00:13:42.289
- And you would watch
this with your uncle?

323
00:13:42.389 --> 00:13:43.625
- Yeah.
Oh. yeah.

324
00:13:43.725 --> 00:13:44.858
Yeah. we loved it.
It was amazing.

325
00:13:44.892 --> 00:13:46.528
We weren't
the only ones. either.

326
00:13:46.594 --> 00:13:48.797
Ian. these other kids.
they were--

327
00:13:48.896 --> 00:13:52.333
they were. like. obsessed.
never missed a bout.

328
00:13:52.433 --> 00:13:54.368
And they'd hang out so much.

329
00:13:54.435 --> 00:13:56.236
some of these fighters would
get to know them.

330
00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:57.938
They would take them
under their wing.

331
00:13:58.005 --> 00:14:01.141
- Hey. TARU was able to pull
some emails from Ian's tablet.

332
00:14:01.241 --> 00:14:03.477
And one in particular
stands out.

333
00:14:03.545 --> 00:14:05.045
The day before
Ian was murdered.

334
00:14:05.112 --> 00:14:07.948
he got this email from
a user named Razor Crazor.

335
00:14:08.015 --> 00:14:10.050
- "Keep your mouth shut.

336
00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:14.121
You tell anyone about us.
and I'll effing kill you."

337
00:14:14.188 --> 00:14:16.156
- Razor.
Oh. yeah. here it is.

338
00:14:16.256 --> 00:14:18.593
Okay. here's a photo
of Ian back in 2002

339
00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.929
with another kid
named Roddy "Razor" Ray.

340
00:15:01.201 --> 00:15:03.605
- Yo. Razor. good fight. man.

341
00:15:03.671 --> 00:15:04.873
- Appreciate it. kid.

342
00:15:04.938 --> 00:15:06.708
- Hey. you're Roddy Ray?

343
00:15:06.775 --> 00:15:08.610
- It's Razor.
Why?

344
00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:09.943
- We're police.

345
00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:12.346
We want to talk to you
about Ian Larkin.

346
00:15:12.413 --> 00:15:14.014
- Yeah. I don't have
anything to say about him.

347
00:15:14.047 --> 00:15:16.049
- Oh. yeah?
Why's that?

348
00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:18.653
- Because the dude is a
crackhead who can't be trusted.

349
00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:21.455
- Is that why you
threatened him?

350
00:15:21.488 --> 00:15:23.090
- I don't know what
you're talking about.

351
00:15:23.157 --> 00:15:25.025
- "Keep your mouth shut.
or I'll kill you"?

352
00:15:25.058 --> 00:15:26.493
Sounds pretty
threatening to me.

353
00:15:26.594 --> 00:15:28.362
- Yeah. me too.

354
00:15:28.429 --> 00:15:32.499
- So where were you
last Friday night?

355
00:15:32.567 --> 00:15:34.435
- Home. half a case deep.

356
00:15:34.536 --> 00:15:35.770
Why?

357
00:15:35.837 --> 00:15:37.906
- Because Ian Larkin is dead.

358
00:15:41.275 --> 00:15:43.645
- You know anything
about that. Razor?

359
00:15:46.213 --> 00:15:47.715
How'd you get those scratches?

360
00:15:50.484 --> 00:15:52.520
- Did you not just see us jack
each other up in the ring?

361
00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:53.888
- Yeah. I know.

362
00:15:53.954 --> 00:15:55.523
But they could be from
a different kind of fight.

363
00:15:55.557 --> 00:15:57.725
- Do you mind telling
us why you wanted

364
00:15:57.792 --> 00:15:59.259
Ian to keep his mouth shut?

365
00:15:59.359 --> 00:16:00.695
- It's private.

366
00:16:00.795 --> 00:16:02.296
- Okay.
We can keep a secret.

367
00:16:02.396 --> 00:16:03.631
- I said it's none
of your business.

368
00:16:03.731 --> 00:16:04.666
- Hey. buddy why
don't you calm down?

369
00:16:04.732 --> 00:16:06.133
- Do not touch me!
No.

370
00:16:06.166 --> 00:16:07.968
- Not your best move.
- I said not to touch me.

371
00:16:08.035 --> 00:16:09.704
- Get your hands
behind your back.

372
00:16:11.606 --> 00:16:13.875
- It doesn't matter how
many times you guys ask.

373
00:16:13.974 --> 00:16:15.543
I didn't kill Ian.
- We heard you.

374
00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:17.679
But there's still an issue
of you not having an alibi.

375
00:16:17.745 --> 00:16:20.815
- Well. not to mention you
threatening to kill Ian.

376
00:16:20.882 --> 00:16:22.082
- I didn't mean it like that.

377
00:16:22.149 --> 00:16:23.818
- Okay.
How did you mean it?

378
00:16:23.885 --> 00:16:25.018
- You never told
one of your friends

379
00:16:25.085 --> 00:16:26.453
you were going to kill him?

380
00:16:26.521 --> 00:16:28.322
I mean. I've known
the kid for a long time.

381
00:16:28.388 --> 00:16:31.826
- All right. so you two met
as fans of the Bruiser fights.

382
00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:33.728
So tell me about that.

383
00:16:33.795 --> 00:16:36.163
- It was fine.
Good. I guess.

384
00:16:36.263 --> 00:16:37.866
- Spend a lot of time
with the fighters?

385
00:16:37.932 --> 00:16:39.901
- Yeah. every chance we could--

386
00:16:39.968 --> 00:16:42.904
after an event.
in the gym. wherever.

387
00:16:42.971 --> 00:16:45.239
I mean. we wanted to learn.

388
00:16:45.272 --> 00:16:47.341
So you did what they told you.

389
00:16:47.407 --> 00:16:49.744
And maybe some of the guys
took it a little too far.

390
00:16:49.844 --> 00:16:54.649
but they were stars.

391
00:16:54.749 --> 00:16:56.316
That's just the way it was.

392
00:16:56.416 --> 00:16:57.484
- And Ian?

393
00:16:57.552 --> 00:16:59.219
- Soft.

394
00:16:59.286 --> 00:17:02.189
Yeah. he couldn't
handle things.

395
00:17:02.289 --> 00:17:05.693
- Like. what kind of things?

396
00:17:05.727 --> 00:17:08.963
- Being messed with.

397
00:17:09.062 --> 00:17:10.798
- Okay. well. that's
a lot of responsibility

398
00:17:10.832 --> 00:17:13.668
for a teenage boy. right.

399
00:17:13.768 --> 00:17:16.270
who's hanging out
with their idols.

400
00:17:16.336 --> 00:17:18.438
guys that were heroes to them.

401
00:17:18.506 --> 00:17:21.441
- It's also an easy way for
kids to get taken advantage of.

402
00:17:23.011 --> 00:17:24.612
- You ever see that happen?

403
00:17:29.349 --> 00:17:33.453
You know. Razor.
childhood victims often carry

404
00:17:33.521 --> 00:17:35.890
the weight of the abuse. right?

405
00:17:35.924 --> 00:17:37.959
They carry the shame.

406
00:17:38.026 --> 00:17:41.261
But the only one that
should feel shame

407
00:17:41.328 --> 00:17:43.698
are the adults
that betrayed them.

408
00:17:43.765 --> 00:17:46.166
the adults that failed them.

409
00:17:49.102 --> 00:17:54.008
- You know. at first.
it was like a dream.

410
00:17:54.107 --> 00:17:57.077
getting to watch
those guys train and fight.

411
00:17:57.177 --> 00:18:00.715
You get close to them.
you know.

412
00:18:00.782 --> 00:18:04.552
maybe share a couple drinks.
smoke some pot.

413
00:18:04.652 --> 00:18:06.955
- It makes you feel
older. important?

414
00:18:07.021 --> 00:18:08.355
- Yeah.

415
00:18:11.025 --> 00:18:14.662
That line gets blurred.

416
00:18:14.762 --> 00:18:16.330
- Yeah.

417
00:18:16.396 --> 00:18:21.803
- One of them starts getting
physical and touching.

418
00:18:21.869 --> 00:18:24.005
It felt gross.

419
00:18:24.104 --> 00:18:26.273
- So that's what predators do.

420
00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:31.079
They make things that
should feel really scary.

421
00:18:31.144 --> 00:18:34.916
they make those things normal.

422
00:18:38.418 --> 00:18:42.657
Can you tell us his name.
your abuser?

423
00:18:46.761 --> 00:18:49.664
- Clint.

424
00:18:49.731 --> 00:18:52.399
Clint O'Neill.

425
00:18:55.135 --> 00:18:56.938
- And you could come forward

426
00:18:57.038 --> 00:19:00.808
and hold O'Neill accountable
for what he did.

427
00:19:00.908 --> 00:19:03.544
- Screw that.

428
00:19:03.645 --> 00:19:05.079
I'd be a joke.

429
00:19:05.178 --> 00:19:06.446
I'm not about to throw
my career away for this.

430
00:19:06.514 --> 00:19:08.448
- Razor. you spend Sunday
nights at a VFW.

431
00:19:08.516 --> 00:19:10.283
bleeding
for a few dozen people.

432
00:19:10.384 --> 00:19:12.086
What are you really losing?

433
00:19:12.185 --> 00:19:13.755
- This doesn't seem
like much to you.

434
00:19:13.788 --> 00:19:16.423
but fighting.

435
00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:19.393
this is the only thing that
I have ever been good at.

436
00:19:26.901 --> 00:19:28.435
- I don't understand.

437
00:19:29.504 --> 00:19:34.374
He--he had never said anything
to me or to our parents.

438
00:19:34.474 --> 00:19:38.445
- So it's very common
for victims of sexual abuse.

439
00:19:38.478 --> 00:19:40.014
- Especially young men.

440
00:19:42.182 --> 00:19:45.520
- I--I resented him.

441
00:19:45.586 --> 00:19:47.889
I treated him like
he was a burden.

442
00:19:47.955 --> 00:19:49.824
Oh. my God.
what's wrong with me?

443
00:19:49.891 --> 00:19:52.894
I was so relieved when
I found out he was dead.

444
00:19:52.994 --> 00:19:54.629
- Shawn. you didn't know.

445
00:19:54.696 --> 00:19:56.130
- Well. I should have.

446
00:19:56.229 --> 00:19:58.265
I'm his brother.

447
00:19:58.298 --> 00:20:00.367
If I had seen something.
I could have helped.

448
00:20:00.434 --> 00:20:01.736
- You can't think like that.

449
00:20:01.836 --> 00:20:03.403
- It's my fault.

450
00:20:03.504 --> 00:20:05.840
I got him into
all that fight crap.

451
00:20:05.907 --> 00:20:08.609
Buds of mine used to hang
around those shows.

452
00:20:08.676 --> 00:20:10.978
- Do you remember
any of their names?

453
00:20:11.079 --> 00:20:12.446
- Probably.

454
00:20:12.513 --> 00:20:14.515
- Okay. well. we would
love to take a look

455
00:20:14.582 --> 00:20:17.250
at Ian's belongings. if you
have any of them in case--

456
00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:18.720
- Yeah. yeah.

457
00:20:18.853 --> 00:20:21.089
Ian got off probation last year
and moved in with Dad and me.

458
00:20:21.155 --> 00:20:23.490
And Ian left most
of his stuff behind.

459
00:20:23.524 --> 00:20:24.726
- Oh.

460
00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:27.595
- Yeah. I get it.

461
00:20:27.695 --> 00:20:28.796
Okay. thanks.

462
00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:30.064
- Didn't go well?

463
00:20:30.131 --> 00:20:31.498
- I spoke with Shawn's
high school friends.

464
00:20:31.599 --> 00:20:33.868
All three spent lots
of time with the fighters.

465
00:20:33.935 --> 00:20:35.302
including O'Neill.

466
00:20:35.335 --> 00:20:37.304
One moved away.
refuses to talk to me.

467
00:20:37.337 --> 00:20:38.906
The other insists.
never saw anything.

468
00:20:39.006 --> 00:20:40.273
- And the third?

469
00:20:40.307 --> 00:20:41.843
- Jumped off a bridge
ten years ago.

470
00:20:41.876 --> 00:20:44.344
- Okay. so O'Neill's alibi
is holding up.

471
00:20:44.377 --> 00:20:46.948
He had dinner with
Sharon Huntsinger. his agent.

472
00:20:47.014 --> 00:20:48.616
and two other people.

473
00:20:48.716 --> 00:20:51.686
And the GPS from his phone
shows that he went

474
00:20:51.786 --> 00:20:54.421
straight back to his hotel.
which was clear across town

475
00:20:54.454 --> 00:20:56.057
from where Ian was found.
- All right.

476
00:20:56.124 --> 00:20:57.290
So if it is O'Neill.
we're going to need

477
00:20:57.357 --> 00:20:58.726
more to connect him to Ian.

478
00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:00.595
- Well. we went through
Ian's belongings.

479
00:21:00.661 --> 00:21:02.063
It's mostly junk.

480
00:21:02.096 --> 00:21:04.732
But we found a journal
from around 2005.

481
00:21:04.766 --> 00:21:09.904
He--he writes about
the abuse in detail.

482
00:21:10.004 --> 00:21:12.305
locations where it happened.

483
00:21:12.372 --> 00:21:15.510
specific acts that he was
forced to be engaged in.

484
00:21:15.576 --> 00:21:17.745
He even talks about
how he blamed himself.

485
00:21:17.812 --> 00:21:20.615
But he never names
O'Neill outright.

486
00:21:20.648 --> 00:21:23.350
- Also turned up records
of a payment Ian received

487
00:21:23.416 --> 00:21:26.154
around the same time
for a whopping 1.200 bucks.

488
00:21:26.254 --> 00:21:28.689
labeled as "reimbursement
for expenses."

489
00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:30.792
- He signed an agreement
for that money.

490
00:21:30.825 --> 00:21:33.326
one with confidentiality.
non-disparagement clauses.

491
00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:35.428
and release of any claim
to litigation.

492
00:21:35.462 --> 00:21:37.464
- Who was the agreement with?

493
00:21:37.532 --> 00:21:41.969
- A defunct LLC called
Fight Reps. Incorporated.

494
00:21:42.036 --> 00:21:44.371
owned by Sharon Huntsinger.

495
00:21:44.471 --> 00:21:45.573
- His agent.
- Yeah.

496
00:21:45.606 --> 00:21:47.041
- Makes this interesting.

497
00:21:50.343 --> 00:21:51.846
- Correspondence
with a law firm.

498
00:21:51.979 --> 00:21:55.049
- From last year.

499
00:21:55.116 --> 00:21:57.251
Ian wanted to sue
Sharon and O'Neill.

500
00:21:57.350 --> 00:21:58.786
- According
to the lawyer's notes.

501
00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:01.189
Huntsinger flipped out
when the firm contacted

502
00:22:01.222 --> 00:22:02.557
her over a potential suit.

503
00:22:02.657 --> 00:22:05.392
- So maybe if Ian
popped up recently.

504
00:22:05.492 --> 00:22:07.128
she wanted to keep him quiet.

505
00:22:07.261 --> 00:22:08.930
- Or cover it up.

506
00:22:08.963 --> 00:22:12.733
Look. I am going to give
Sharon a little squeeze

507
00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:15.870
and see just how loyal
she is to her favorite client.

508
00:22:19.372 --> 00:22:22.276
- I'd be lying if I said
the fight game was easy.

509
00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:23.878
People would rather
watch inbreds slap

510
00:22:23.945 --> 00:22:25.246
each other on the internet.

511
00:22:25.313 --> 00:22:27.782
But I managed to keep
the place afloat.

512
00:22:27.849 --> 00:22:30.251
Might not be
fancy offices on Park Ave.

513
00:22:30.284 --> 00:22:33.588
